  <article class="page">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">BIRD approach paper</p>
      <h1>Everything Is a Semantic Integration</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">An output-led approach to evolving BIRD’s shared semantic core</p>
      <span class="status">Status: Approach paper for discussion</span>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="abstract" aria-labelledby="abstract-title">
        <h2 id="abstract-title">Abstract</h2>
        <p>Every new reporting requirement should enter BIRD as a semantic integration problem, and every successful integration should leave the BIRD semantic core stronger and less redundant than before.</p>
        <p>BIRD already applies this principle when it maps non-reference regulatory dictionaries, such as the EBA dictionary, to the reference SDD dictionary. These mappings transform externally defined reporting requirements into reference annotated templates and Reference Output Layer structures. In other words, BIRD mappings are already semantic integrations.<sup><a href="#ref-1" aria-label="Reference 1">1</a></sup></p>
        <p>This paper proposes extending that principle. Semantically integrated concepts discovered through output-layer mappings should not remain permanently outside the BIRD input model. Where they represent stable, reusable and sufficiently granular business meaning, they should progressively be promoted into the BIRD semantic core and reflected natively in the Input Layer or Enriched Input Layer. Existing overlapping or weaker input concepts can then be mapped, migrated and ultimately retired.</p>
        <p>The result is an <strong>output-led, core-over-time</strong> approach to BIRD development.</p>
      </section>

      <nav aria-label="Contents">
        <h2>Contents</h2>
        <ol>
